  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 AM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nA southerly low-level jet will move into the area today, reaching \nabout 30-40 knots at 850mb as it passes overhead. These winds are \nexpected to mix to the surface late this morning through the early \nafternoon over central and north central South Dakota, so gusts up \nto 40 miles per hour will be possible. As the low-level jet \nprogresses eastward, it is expected to diminish, keeping the wind \nthreat contained to mainly to counties bordering the Missouri River \ntoday. The low-level jet will also be responsible for developing \nsome light rain showers to perhaps weak thunderstorms, which will \nprogress from southwest to northeast across the forecast area this \nmorning to early afternoon. Expecting little in the way of rainfall \naccumulation from these showers, perhaps a few hundredths at best. \n\nThursday into Friday, a Colorado Low will move up into the Northern \nPlains, bringing heavy rain into the Aberdeen forecast area. The \nbulk of the heavy rain is expected to fall overnight Thursday into \nFriday morning. Over the past 24 hours, precipitation totals have \ntrended upward, particularly over eastern and central South Dakota. \nThrough the event, ensemble median values now sit around 2\"-3\" over \nmuch of eastern South Dakota, up from around 1\"-2\" 24 hours ago. \nSimilar increases are noted over central South Dakota, now sitting \naround 1\"-2\" medians. This increase is not necessarily attributed to \na northerly shift to the track of the storms. While there was a very \nslight shift northward, the axis of heaviest rainfall still remains \nto the south over the Sioux Falls CWA. Other ensembles tend to agree \nwith these precip totals, and show perhaps additional signal for a \nshift further north of the highest rainfall totals. Once again the \nGEFS shows significantly lower rainfall totals than other models, \nwith medians less than an inch across the entire forecast area. \nGiven that this phenomena was present during the most recent \nsynoptically forced system and did not verify very well, confidence \nin these low end totals out of the GEFS is quite low at this time. \nModels spreads still sit at around 1\"-2\" over most of the area, so \nstill a bit of variability at the moment. \n\nWith these high rainfall totals, it is worth looking at the flooding \npotential with this event. A fully saturated profile with PWATs well \nabove the 90th percentile (widespread PWATs around 1.75\" for this \nevent) prove favorable, as does the deep warm cloud layer of 11-12k \nfeet. Models are resolving storm motion to be around 10-20 knots, \nwhich while not overly favorable, does provide some additional \nsupport for storms lingering over the same area for an extended \nperiod, especially on the lower end of storm motion speeds. The main \nquestion will therefore be the availability of instability in the \narea. Models still don't resolve much, reaching 500-1000 J/kg of \nMUCAPE at best. Still, given the other variables in play, this weak \ninstability may be enough to realize the flooding threat. As such, \nthere is a Slight to Marginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall Thursday \nnight into Friday morning (when the heaviest rainfall totals are \nexpected) and a Slight to Marginal Risk again over eastern South \nDakota and western Minnesota for Friday morning after sunrise. \n\nBehind the low, an nupper-level trough will progress eastward from \nthe Pacific Northwest to over the Northern Plains. This will allow \nfor a cooler airmass to take hold, pushing temperatures back down \nbelow normal over the weekend. Additional rain is likely as the \nupper-level low moves in. Beyond this weekend, model guidance shows \nsome disagreement on how the pattern will evolve, but in general \nprobabilities favor temperatures returning to near-normal.",
